rkWorkspaceChanged(revision: number): void`\n  - `admit(tool: string, args: unknown): Admission`\n  - `complete(ticket: string, outcome: Outcome): void`\n  - `snapshot(): Snapshot`\n\nInitial state: `{turnId:null, revision:0, admissions:0, pending:0, closed:true}`.\n- Before an active turn, `admit` returns `turn_closed`.\n- `beginTurn` requires a nonempty string after trimming; store the trimmed ID.\n- An invalid ID throws `RangeError` with no state change.\n- Every successful `beginTurn` creates a fresh generation, even if the visible ID repeats.\n- Reset revision, admissions, pending calls, and outcome history; set `closed:false`.\n- Tickets are unique within the instance across all generations and never reused.\n- External randomness is unnecessary.\n\n### R02 - budgets, outcomes, denial precedence\n- `MAX_ADMISSIONS = 12` per turn.\n- `MAX_TRANSIENT_FAILURES = 2` per signature and workspace revision.\n- Every allowed admission immediately consumes one turn admission, including calls later cancelled or failed.\n- Denied calls consume none.\n- Completion does not refund admissions.\n- Admission 12 is allowed if otherwise eligible; admission 13 is `budget_exhausted`.\n\nFor a signature at its admitted revision:\n- While pending, another matching call is `in_flight`.\n- `ok`, `no_matches`, and `permanent_error` are terminal; another matching call is `repeat_terminal`.\n- `transient_error` increments a failure count. One failure permits another call; two deny another call as `retry_exhausted`.\n- `cancelled` releases pending but creates no terminal result or transient failure. It still consumed an admission; retry is eligible if other limits permit it.\n\nEach ticket completes at most once. Unknown, completed, or superseded-generation tickets are ignored without state changes. Invalid outcomes passed at runtime for a live ticket throw `TypeError` without changing state.\n\nDenial precedence: `turn_closed`, `invalid_call`, `in_flight`, `repeat_terminal`, `retry_exhausted`, `budget_exhausted`.\n\n### R07 - canonical signatures\n- A signature combines the trimmed, case-sensitive tool name and canonical JSON arguments.\n- Names must be nonempty strings after trimming.\n- Root args must be a plain object with `Object.prototype` or null as its prototype.\n\nAccept:\n- null, booleans, finite numbers, strings, dense arrays of accepted data, and plain objects containing accepted data.\n\nSort object keys recursively by JavaScript string/code-unit order; property order must not change signatures. Array order does matter.\n\nUse JSON string/number semantics: `-0` equals `0`.\n\nPreserve string case, whitespace, escapes, and Unicode normalization form.\n\nNever rewrite shell commands to make them appear equivalent.\n\nReject:\n- nonfinite numbers, undefined, functions, bigint, symbol keys/values, class instances, Date/Map/Set, array holes, cycles, accessor properties, and non-enumerable own properties.\n\nArrays' ordinary length property is permitted; extra array properties are rejected.\n\nNever invoke getters.\n\nRepeated references in separate non-cyclic locations are allowed.\n\nTreat `__proto__` and `constructor` as ordinary own data keys; never mutate prototypes.\n\nInvalid calls return `invalid_call` without throwing, issuing a ticket, or consuming budget.\n\nValidate nested data as well as roots.\n\n### R18 - workspace changes and stale completions\n- Only the caller knows a relevant workspace change occurred.\n- The guard never advances revisions on its own.\n- `markWorkspaceChanged` accepts a nonnegative safe integer.\n- Smaller revisions, negatives, or non-safe-integers throw `RangeError` without state changes.\n- Same revision is a no-op; larger revision advances it.\n- Calling without an active turn throws `Error` without state changes.\n\nTerminal history and transient limits belong to the **revision at admission**. Advancing revision permits previously terminal or retry-exhausted signatures, subject to remaining turn budget.\n\nPending duplicate protection is **turn-wide**, across revisions. A change cannot admit the same signature while an old-revision call is pending. Completing the old call releases it, but its history remains at its old revision; it cannot poison the new revision.\n\nRevision changes do not reset/increase the 12-admission budget.\n\n`beginTurn` creates a new generation; previous-generation callbacks cannot change it, even with reused turn IDs or signatures.\n\n### R23 - isolation, checks, deliverable\n- `snapshot` returns a copy; modifying it cannot change the guard.\n- Admissions appear immediately after reservation.\n- Pending decreases exactly once at completion and never goes negative.\n- No filesystem IO, networking, timers, external dependencies, model requests, or mutable global state shared between instances.\n- Keep the compiler configuration.\n- Add meaningful regression assertions, not merely file-existence checks.\n- The successful artifact blocks duplicated pending calls and unchanged terminal/empty results, permits a retry after one transient failure, respects total budget, and handles revision changes/stale callbacks.\n- RESULT.md must cite R02, R07, and R18 for the three requested contract decisions.\n\n## Design\n\nLet me think through the data structures and algorithm.\n\n### State\n```typescript\nclass ToolLoopGuard {\n  private turnId: string | null = null;\n  private revision: number = 0;\n  private admissions: number = 0;  // count of admissions in current turn\n  private pending: number = 0;  // count of pending calls\n  private closed: boolean = true;\n  private generation: number = 0;  // increments on each beginTurn\n  \n  // ticket -> { tool, args (canonical), revision, outcome, generation }\n  private tickets: Map<string, TicketInfo> = new Map();\n  \n  // signature -> entry (at a specific revision)\n  // signature = tool + canonical args\n  // entry = { revision, pending (boolean), terminalOutcome (or null), transientFailures }\n  private signatures: Map<string, SignatureEntry> = new Map();\n  \n  private ticketCounter: number = 0;\n}\n```\n\nWait, I need to think more carefully about the signature history and revision handling.\n\n### Signature key\nThe signature is `tool` (trimmed, case-sensitive) + canonical JSON of args.\n\nThe canonical JSON needs to:\n- Sort object keys recursively by JS string/code-unit order\n- Handle -0 == 0\n- Preserve string case, whitespace, escapes, Unicode normalization form\n- Reject nonfinite numbers, undefined, functions, bigint, symbol keys/values, class instances, Date/Map/Set, array holes, cycles, accessor properties, non-enumerable own properties\n- Arrays' ordinary length property is permitted; extra array properties are rejected\n- Never invoke getters\n- Repeated references in separate non-cyclic locations are allowed\n- Treat __proto__ and constructor as ordinary own data keys; never mutate prototypes\n\n### Canonical JSON algorithm\nThis is the tricky part. Let me think about this.\n\nI need to:\n1. Validate the value recursively\n2. Produce a canonical string representation\n\nFor the canonical form:\n- null -> \"null\"\n- boolean -> \"true\" or \"false\"\n- string -> JSON.stringify with proper escaping\n- number -> JSON.stringify (handles -0 vs 0? JSON.stringify(-0) is \"0\", JSON.stringify(0) is \"0\", so yes -0 == 0)\n- array -> \"[item1,item2,...]\" with items in order\n- plain object -> \"{\"key1\":value1,\"key2\":value2,...}\" with keys sorted\n\nWait, but I need to handle -0 == 0. JSON.stringify(-0) gives \"0\" and JSON.stringify(0) gives \"0\", so that's handled by using JSON.stringify for numbers.\n\nActually, let me think about whether I should use JSON.stringify at all or build my own.\n\nThe issue with JSON.stringify is:\n- It would invoke getters if I pass an object with accessors\n- It would use the object's property order, not sorted order\n- It might not properly reject non-plain objects\n\nLet me think about what I need to do:\n\nFor a plain object, I need to:\n1. Check it's a plain object (Object.prototype or null as prototype)\n2. Get own enumerable property keys (but not via for...in which would include inherited)\n3. Check each property is a data property, not an accessor\n4. Check no non-enumerable own properties... wait, the contract says</think>Now I have a clear picture. Let me analyze the contract and plan the first concrete edits.\n\n**First concrete edits:**\n- Rewrite `src/tool-loop-guard.ts`: implement `MAX_ADMISSIONS=12`, `MAX_TRANSIENT_FAILURES=2`, canonical-signature validator/serializer (R07), generation-scoped state with per-(signature,revision) terminal/transient tracking (R02, R18), and `snapshot()` returning a copy (R23).\n- Add regression tests under `tests/` (in addition to the retained public smoke test) covering duplicate-pending, transient retry, budget exhaustion, revision staleness, and invalid-call handling.\n- Write `RESULT.md` citing R02, R07, R18.\n\nLet me now write the implementation.<tool_call>write<arg_key>i</arg_key><arg_value>Implementing the full ToolLoopGuard</arg_value><arg_key>path</arg_key><arg_value>C:/code/poolside-strata/performance/tool-call-investigation/scratch/src/tool-loop-guard.ts</arg_value><arg_key>content</arg_key><arg_value>export const MAX_ADMISSIONS: number = 12;\nexport const MAX_TRANSIENT_FAILURES: number = 2;\nexport type Outcome = \"ok\" | \"no_matches\" | \"transient_error\" | \"permanent_error\" | \"cancelled\";\nexport type DenialReason = \"turn_closed\" | \"invalid_call\" | \"in_flight\" | \"repeat_terminal\" | \"retry_exhausted\" | \"budget_exhausted\";\nexport type Admission = { allowed: true; ticket: string } | { allowed: false; reason: DenialReason };\nexport interface Snapshot {\n  turnId: string | null;\n  revision: number;\n  admissions: number;\n  pending: number;\n  closed: boolean;\n}\n\ntype TicketInfo = {\n  generation: number;\n  tool: string;\n  signature: string;\n  revision: number;\n  completed: boolean;\n};\n\ntype 